WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.390 --> 00:00:03.480
- Haven't you ever had
those times in your life

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:07.830
where you just feel all
consumed with tasting something

00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:10.720
again that you haven't
had in years and years?

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:14.340
That's really what I'm
doing here is bringing back

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:17.660
those incredible desserts,
those incredible experiences

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:19.223
that have gone extinct.

00:00:24.970 --> 00:00:28.650
I put myself through this
process of resurrecting desserts

00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:31.660
because I truly feel that a good dessert

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:33.360
can change the course of your day.

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:39.160
I'm Valerie Gordon, owner
of Valerie Confections,

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:41.853
and I'm also a dessert anthropologist.

00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:47.130
The way this project of
resurrecting historic

00:00:47.130 --> 00:00:51.440
desserts began was in 2009 the food editor

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:54.650
from the Los Angeles
Times magazine asked me

00:00:54.650 --> 00:00:57.470
to create a Blum's Koffee Krunch Cake

00:00:57.470 --> 00:00:59.460
for an article that she was doing.

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:02.340
And it was this amazing
full-circle moment,

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:04.380
because I grew up with that cake;

00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:07.560
I know that cake, and I
missed that cake terribly.

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:12.550
Blum's was an incredible
bakery and cafe in California.

00:01:12.550 --> 00:01:13.970
There were eight locations.

00:01:13.970 --> 00:01:16.150
The last one closed in the ’80s.

00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:19.150
When the article came
out, we literally received

00:01:19.150 --> 00:01:23.877
125 phone calls from
people going, "Oh my God,

00:01:23.877 --> 00:01:26.013
"I want this cake."

00:01:26.013 --> 00:01:28.850
The desserts that I've resurrected to date

00:01:28.850 --> 00:01:32.520
are, of course the Koffee
Krunch Cake from Blum's.

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:36.770
I've brought back the Banana
Shortcake from Chasen's,

00:01:36.770 --> 00:01:39.460
the Brown Derby Grapefruit
Cake is another cake

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:42.320
that I've resurrected, and now soon to be

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:44.653
the Cof-fiesta sundae, as well.

00:01:47.230 --> 00:01:51.950
The anthropology of the
dessert resurrection

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:56.248
is really one of the most
interesting parts of all of this.

00:01:56.248 --> 00:02:00.510
I start the research process
at the central library.

00:02:00.510 --> 00:02:04.630
There is a room, and this
is a very well-guarded room,

00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:06.043
called the Menu Vault.

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:10.933
There are menus that
date back for 150 years.

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:15.910
I find that looking at the actual menus

00:02:15.910 --> 00:02:18.630
is so much more gratifying
than just pulling something

00:02:18.630 --> 00:02:20.640
up online because you're really getting

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:22.370
to the heart and soul of the place.

00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:26.690
You're almost transported
into that place at that time

00:02:26.690 --> 00:02:29.330
by holding something
that these people held.

00:02:29.330 --> 00:02:30.223
You really are.

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:34.980
Then I email a bunch of people,
or I call a bunch of people

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:38.380
who were natives to Los
Angeles, or whatever city

00:02:38.380 --> 00:02:42.470
the bakery was in to find out,
hey, do you remember this?

00:02:42.470 --> 00:02:43.303
Do you remember that?

00:02:43.303 --> 00:02:45.040
- We always used to call
this the adult sundae

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:46.480
because this is the one my mom would eat.

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:47.380
- This is the grownup sundae.

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:50.760
Can they describe the
texture of a whipped cream?

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:53.750
Can they tell me whether there
was vanilla in it, or not?

00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:57.710
- I'm pretty sure they
did one basic recipe

00:02:57.710 --> 00:03:00.460
for whipped cream, and
then flavored accordingly.

00:03:00.460 --> 00:03:02.750
It was little kid whipped cream.

00:03:02.750 --> 00:03:04.187
- One cherry.
- Yes.

00:03:04.187 --> 00:03:05.640
- This is amazing.

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:08.223
Like, you have been indispensable.

00:03:10.790 --> 00:03:13.200
I'm not particularly interested in retro.

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:16.420
It is about reliving those sense memories.

00:03:16.420 --> 00:03:18.410
It's sort of like when you hear a song

00:03:18.410 --> 00:03:21.720
that was your favorite song from 1982,

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:24.870
you're really thrown
back into that moment.

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:29.750
It's become this very
emotional, very fulfilling

00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:32.853
part of my job to bring
these cakes back for people.

